1. WHAT ASTIR IS
================

1.1  What is Astir?

  An APRS client for amateur radio.  It listens to a radio or to the internet,
  decodes what other stations are transmitting -- positions, weather, messages,
  telemetry -- and draws them on a map.

  See README.md.


1.2  How is it different from Xastir?

  Astir is a hard fork of Xastir with a GTK4 front end and a core that links
  with no toolkit at all.  Xastir is built around X11 and Motif, and the two
  were not separable from it: the toolkit reached into the APRS parser, the
  shapefile reader and the map drivers.

  In practice, what you notice is that symbols and maps are vector-drawn and
  stay sharp at any zoom and on any display scale, that labels are placed rather
  than stamped so a crowded city stays readable, and that the program is
  genuinely idle when nothing is happening.

  What you also notice is that a lot of Xastir's features are not reachable
  here yet.  See 1.4.

  FORK.md has the full reasoning.


1.3  Can I run both?  Will it touch my Xastir setup?

  Yes, and no.  This is a requirement of the project, not an aspiration.

  Different binary (`astir`), different data directory (`/usr/share/astir`),
  different settings directory (`~/.astir`), different environment variables
  (`ASTIR_*`).  Astir never reads or writes anything of Xastir's.  Staging an
  install and diffing every path against the ones the Xastir package owns finds
  zero collisions.

  Astir will refuse to start if you point `ASTIR_DATA_BASE` at another
  program's data directory, rather than quietly rendering with somebody else's
  styling rules.

  `~/.astir` is its own directory and is not a reinterpretation of `~/.xastir`.
  Copying one to the other is not supported and is a good way to end up
  debugging a problem neither program has.


1.4  Is it finished?

  No, and it is worth knowing what is missing before you invest an evening:

    - It cannot transmit.  Beaconing, messaging and digipeating exist in the
      core and are not reachable from the interface.  Nothing can go on the air.
    - Received messages are decoded and logged, not displayed.
    - There is no settings window beyond interfaces and maps.  Everything else
      is edited in `~/.astir/config/astir.cnf`.
    - GPS, weather alerts and the SQL database backends are in the core and are
      not exposed.

  What does work: receiving from a radio or from APRS-IS, decoding, the map,
  station symbols and trails, station info, the station list, the map chooser
  and interface management.


1.5  Where do I report bugs?

  <https://github.com/queueingqt/Astir/issues>

  Useful things to include: what you did, what happened, what you expected, the
  output printed on the terminal, and the output of `./configure` for your build
  if it is a build problem.  See 4.2 about version numbers -- there is currently
  no good answer, so say which commit you built from if you know it.


2. GETTING IT RUNNING
=====================

2.1  It will not build.

  Read the output of `./configure` first.  The most common causes, in order:

    - GTK4 is missing or older than 4.10.  This is fatal and configure says so
      plainly.  Check with `pkg-config --modversion gtk4`.
    - `bootstrap.sh` failed, so there is no `configure` script and every
      subsequent step fails confusingly.  Its cause is nearly always a missing
      autoconf, automake or libtool.
    - Headers are somewhere the compiler does not look.  Common with libgeotiff,
      and normal on FreeBSD and macOS.  Pass `CPPFLAGS` and `LDFLAGS`.

  INSTALL.md covers all three in detail.


2.2  It starts, but the map is blank.

  Look at the terminal.  If Astir says it is starting with the default offline
  map set and you still see nothing, shapelib support was almost certainly not
  compiled in -- the bundled basemap is shapefiles, so without it there is
  nothing to draw.  Re-run configure and read the summary line for ShapeLib.

  If the map draws but is entirely one colour, you are probably zoomed into the
  middle of an ocean.  Set your position (2.6) and restart.


2.3  The map only shows coastlines and no detail.

  That is the bundled map, working as intended.  It is the Natural Earth
  1:50,000,000 world set -- coastlines, countries, lakes and major rivers -- and
  at a city zoom it genuinely has nothing more to offer.  It is there so that a
  machine which has never been online still draws a real map.

  For street-level detail, add maps.  README.MAPS explains where to get them;
  `tools/osm_import.sh` converts an OpenStreetMap extract into shapefiles Astir
  already knows how to draw, which is the best offline route.


2.4  Maps I installed do not appear in the map chooser.

  The map index is cached.  After adding or removing map files, use
  Menu -> Maps -> Rebuild Map Index, or delete `~/.astir/config/map_index.sys`
  and restart.

  If they appear in the chooser but do not draw, they are indexed but not
  selected -- tick them.  If they are selected and still do not draw, check
  whether the format needs a library you did not build with (GeoTIFF needs
  libgeotiff, raster images need GraphicsMagick, tile servers need libcurl).


2.5  Nothing appears on the map -- no stations at all.

  Astir has no interfaces configured by default, so out of the box it receives
  nothing.  Open Menu -> Connections -> Interfaces (Ctrl+I) and add one.

  The quickest way to prove the receive path works, with no radio, is an
  APRS-IS internet server interface: host `noam.aprs2.net`, port 14580,
  passcode `-1`, and a server filter such as `r/34.05/-118.25/150`.  A passcode
  of `-1` is a receive-only login.

  If you add one and still see nothing:

    - Check the port's status line in the interface window.  It says whether it
      connected.
    - An APRS-IS server with no filter set may send you nothing at all.  Set
      one.
    - You may be receiving stations that are off-screen.  Open the station list
      (Ctrl+L) -- if stations are listed there, reception is fine and the view
      is wrong.


2.6  How do I set my callsign and home position?

  In `~/.astir/config/astir.cnf`, while Astir is not running:

      STATION_CALLSIGN:N0CALL
      SCREEN_LAT:20152269
      SCREEN_LONG:22192914

  There is no settings window yet (5.1).  Astir rewrites this file on clean
  exit, so edit it with the program closed or your changes will be overwritten.


2.7  My settings were not saved.

  Astir writes its config when the application shuts down cleanly.  Closing the
  window, or Ctrl-C in the terminal, both count -- the signal is routed through
  the normal shutdown path for exactly this reason.  A `kill -9`, a crash or a
  power cut will lose changes made since startup.

  It also deliberately does not save if the core failed to start, because
  writing defaults over a config that was probably fine is worse than saving
  nothing.

  If you edited `astir.cnf` by hand while Astir was running, your edits were
  overwritten on exit.  Edit it with the program closed.


3. RADIOS AND INTERFACES
========================

3.1  What can it connect to?

  Four kinds can be created from the interface window:

    Software TNC (AGWPE)      Direwolf, or anything else speaking AGWPE.  This
                              is how a sound-card TNC is reached.
    APRS-IS internet server   The internet side of the APRS network.  No radio.
    Serial KISS TNC           A hardware TNC in KISS mode on a serial port.
    Serial TNC (command mode) An older TNC driven with command strings.

  Other types the core supports -- GPS, kernel AX.25, multi-port KISS -- still
  load and run if they are already in your config file, but cannot yet be
  created through the interface.


3.2  How do I use it with Direwolf?

  Run Direwolf with an AGWPE port -- that is the `AGWPORT 8000` line in
  `direwolf.conf`, and it is on by default in most packages.  Then add a
  Software TNC (AGWPE) interface in Astir pointing at `localhost` port 8000.

  Direwolf does the modem work; Astir does the APRS work.  If Direwolf's own
  output shows packets being decoded and Astir shows nothing, the problem is
  between the two of them -- check that the AGWPE port is actually enabled and
  that nothing else has claimed it.


3.3  Can it transmit?

  No.

  There is an "Allow transmit on this interface" checkbox, off by default, and
  it arms a path that nothing currently originates traffic on.  Beaconing and
  messaging are not reachable from this front end.

  If you need to transmit today, use Xastir.  That is not a dodge -- the two
  install side by side specifically so that this is a reasonable answer.


3.4  I get "permission denied" on an AX.25 interface.

  Kernel AX.25 ports need elevated privileges:

      sudo chmod 4755 /usr/local/bin/astir

  This only applies if you built with `--with-ax25`.  Nothing else in Astir
  needs privileges, and you should not run it as root.


3.5  What is APZ, and why is it in my packets?

  It is the destination field -- the "tocall" -- that identifies the software
  that sent a packet.  Xastir's is `APX`, which is registered to Xastir.

  Astir is not Xastir, so emitting `APX` would tell every station on the air
  something false and impossible for the receiver to debug.  `APZ` is the prefix
  the APRS specification reserves for experimental and unregistered software,
  which is exactly what this is until a tocall is applied for.

  Since Astir cannot transmit yet (3.3), you will only see this in your own
  configuration, not on the air.


4. RUNNING IT
=============

4.1  Can I run more than one copy at once?

  Yes, if you give each one its own settings directory:

      ASTIR_USER_BASE=~/astir-test astir

  Two copies sharing `~/.astir` will fight over the config file, and the one
  that exits last wins.  This is also the right way to try something out
  without risking a working setup.


4.2  Which version am I running?

  There is currently no good way to tell, and that is a known gap.  The About
  dialog does not show a version, and this front end does not parse `-V` -- it
  hands its arguments straight to GTK.  The build stamp is generated on every
  build and then read by nothing.

  For now, if you built it yourself, `git describe --dirty` in the source tree
  is the answer.


4.3  Why does it use no CPU when nothing is happening?

  Because that is correct, and it is deliberate.

  Astir does not poll.  The main loop sleeps until something actually happens:
  an arriving packet wakes it through a file descriptor, ports and stations
  announce their own changes, and the map redraws on the compositor's frame
  clock rather than on a timer.  An idle Astir really is idle.

  If you were expecting steady background CPU because that is what APRS clients
  usually do, nothing is wrong.


4.4  It draws slowly.

  Usually too many maps selected at too much detail.  A world view trying to
  draw every street in a county is doing an enormous amount of work that nobody
  can see.

    - Deselect maps you are not using, in the map chooser.
    - Check the zoom ranges on your maps.  Shapefile detail levels come from
      the dbfawk rules; see README.MAPS.
    - Raster tiles are cached decoded in memory, so the second draw is much
      faster than the first.  If every draw is slow, you are probably
      re-reading vector data, not tiles.

  `ASTIR_PERF=1` prints per-operation timings and geometry counters on exit,
  which will tell you which of these it is rather than leaving you to guess.


4.5  Can I change the language?

  Partly.  The language files ship and the `LANGUAGE` setting in `astir.cnf` is
  honoured, but there is no way to change it from the interface, and the GTK4
  front end's own strings -- menus, the interface window, the station list --
  are not translated.  So a non-English setting gets you a partly translated
  program.


4.6  Does it run on Windows or macOS?

  It has never been built or run on either.

  macOS is plausible -- GTK4 and all the map libraries are in Homebrew and
  MacPorts, and the notes in INSTALL.md about headers in unusual places were
  written with it in mind.  Nobody has tried.

  Windows would mean MSYS2 rather than Cygwin; see README.CYGWIN, which is
  honest about being a sketch and not a procedure.


5. THINGS IT DOES NOT DO
========================

5.1  Why is there no settings window?

  Because it has not been written yet.  Xastir's configuration lives in Motif
  dialogs which do not come across, and rebuilding all of them was not the first
  priority -- getting the program to receive, decode and draw correctly was.

  Interfaces and map selection have windows because you cannot use the program
  without them.  Everything else is in `~/.astir/config/astir.cnf`, which is
  plain text and reasonably self-describing.


5.2  Where are my received messages?

  Decoded and logged, but not displayed.  The message windows are Motif dialogs
  in Xastir and have no GTK4 equivalent yet.


5.3  Why doesn't Astir have <feature>?

  Most likely because the port has not reached it.  A great deal of Xastir's
  functionality is present in the core and simply has no interface attached --
  which means adding it is usually a matter of writing a window, not writing the
  feature.

  If it matters to you, an issue saying so is genuinely useful for deciding what
  gets built next.  A pull request is more useful still; see CONTRIBUTING.md and
  README.GIT.md.


